[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"LSP: Subtyping, Behavioral Subtyping, and Contract Design"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP) is the third principle of the SOLID acronym. Formulated by Barbara Liskov, it dictates that objects of a superclass should be replaceable with objects of its subclasses without breaking the application's correctness."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Subtyping vs. Behavioral Subtyping"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Nominal subtyping (supported by most object-oriented languages via inheritance) only guarantees structural compatibility. Behavioral subtyping, however, guarantees semantic compatibility. A behavioral subtype must honor the expectations and guarantees established by the supertype."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Contract Design (Design by Contract)"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"To achieve behavioral subtyping, we rely on Design by Contract (DbC), which models methods using three constraints: Preconditions, Postconditions, and Invariants."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Preconditions: Conditions that must be true before a method is executed. Subtypes can weaken preconditions, but never strengthen them."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Postconditions: Conditions that must be true after a method completes. Subtypes can strengthen postconditions, but never weaken them."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Invariants: Conditions that must remain true for the lifetime of the object."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Code Example: Contract Violation"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"The following C# example illustrates an LSP violation through improper handling of preconditions."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"public class Rectangle
{
    public virtual int Width { get; set; }
    public virtual int Height { get; set; }
    public int Area => Width * Height;
}

public class Square : Rectangle
{
    public override int Width
    {
        get => base.Width;
        set { base.Width = value; base.Height = value; }
    }
    public override int Height
    {
        get => base.Height;
        set { base.Width = value; base.Height = value; }
    }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the code above, Square violates LSP because a client expecting a generic Rectangle assumes that setting the width does not affect the height. The Square subtype introduces unexpected side effects, breaking the invariant contract."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Refactoring for LSP Compliance"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"To fix this LSP violation, we should avoid modeling inheritance where behavioral contracts do not align. Instead, we extract a shared abstraction or keep them as distinct types implementing an interface."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"public interface IShape
{
    int Area { get;
    }
}

public class Rectangle : IShape
{
    public int Width { get; } 
    public int Height { get; }
    public Rectangle(int width, int height) { Width = width; Height = height; }
    public int Area => Width * Height;
}

public class Square : IShape
{
    public int Side { get; }
    public Square(int side) { Side = side; }
    public int Area => Side * Side;
}"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"By decoupling Rectangle and Square under an IShape interface, we honor the contract of each specific shape without forcing unwanted inheritance behaviors."}]
